---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const archCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'a*cosh(x/a)', label: 'catenary (pure chain)', color: '#44403c', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: 'a + x^2/(2*a)', label: 'parabola (equal spread of weight)', color: 'rgba(180,35,24,0.65)' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'curvature parameter a', min: 1, max: 6, step: 0.5, value: 3 },
  ],
  x: { min: -8, max: 8, label: 'x (m)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 14, label: 'height (m)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/foundational/form-follows-forces">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Architecture begins with a structural decision disguised as a shape decision. A
    <strong>beam</strong> carries load by <em>bending</em> — one face stretches, the other
    squeezes, and the middle does expensive work. An <strong>arch</strong> carries the same load
    by <em>pure compression</em> — every stone pushing on its neighbour, no pulling anywhere.
    Stone is strong in compression and weak in tension, which is why the Romans, who had no
    steel, built arches that still stand two thousand years later.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the hanging chain and the parabola — nearly identical shapes, subtly different physics" widget="plotter" config={archCfg} />

  <p>
    Flip the first curve upside down and you have the perfect arch: a chain hanging under its
    own weight traces a <strong>catenary</strong>, and an arch following that shape carries pure
    compression with zero bending. The parabola is its cousin — correct when weight is spread
    evenly along the span (a bridge deck) rather than along the curve itself. Gaudí hung chains
    upside down to design the Sagrada Família's columns; Hooke had described the trick in 1675:
    "as hangs the flexible line, so but inverted will stand the rigid arch."
  </p>

  <h2>From arch to skyscraper</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gothic cathedrals</strong> refined the arch into ribs and flying buttresses — stone skeletons whose walls could open into glass.</li>
    <li><strong>Steel changed the game (1880s):</strong> a material strong in tension meant beams could span far and frames could stack — the elevator plus the steel frame made the skyscraper possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Concrete with steel inside</strong> (reinforced concrete, 1890s–1900s) merged both virtues: compression from the concrete, tension from the rebar.</li>
    <li><strong>Shells and catenaries returned</strong> in the 20th century — Félix Candela's thin concrete vaults and the Gateway Arch (1965, a flattened catenary in steel) are the chain's idea at monument scale.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The architectural lesson generalises: <strong>form follows forces</strong> — and the most
    beautiful structures in history are usually the ones where shape and structure stopped
    fighting.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hooke (1675); Allen & Zalewski, Form and Forces; Mainstone, Developments in Structural Form.</p>
</TopicPage>
